# HMD feedback loop

The **HMD feedback loop** is the cycle in which a HyperMarkDown card is
evaluated, the result is written beside the cell that produced it, a person
or agent interprets the result, and an accepted revision becomes the next
version of the card. It is a design proposed in the discussion, not an
established term. The discussion is explicit about its status: it is ordinary
feedback, and becomes a candidate [[strange-loop]] only under a further
condition.

## Description

### The parts

The proposal assigns one job to each piece of the system.

- **Cards** are persistent units of knowledge. They hold prose, links and
  instructions.
- **HQL cells** inside cards are executable computations.
- **Output** is written next to the cell, inside the same document.
- **Git** is the record of successive revisions.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
 A[HMD card: knowledge and instructions] --> B[Execute HQL cell]
 B --> C[Persist result beside cell]
 C --> D[Human or agent interprets result]
 D --> E[Propose and review revision]
 E -->|new revision in Git| A
```

As a formula, with $H_n$ the vault at revision $n$:

$$H_{n+1}=\operatorname{Integrate}(H_n,\operatorname{Eval}(H_n)).$$

### Why this alone is not a strange loop

A cycle of document, code, output and document again is a [[feedback-loop]].
Every stage is the same kind of thing as the next: a text, a result, a new
text. The discussion made this correction twice. An earlier, looser
statement had equated iterative self-modification with strange loops, and was
withdrawn: an HMD card repeatedly rewriting another card need not collapse
any distinction between levels.

The essay on AI-oriented languages draws the same line. Software development
already runs from intention to specification to program to execution to
observation and back to a revised specification. That is the same loop at
larger scale, and it is still only feedback.

### The condition

The loop becomes interesting when two things hold together.

1. A document can represent the mechanism that interprets it: its own cells,
   the rules by which it is evaluated, the criteria by which revisions are
   accepted.
2. The interpretation feeds back into that same mechanism, so that the
   representation takes part in changing what it represents.

The stated scientific target is "reflective level crossing, rather than
recursive execution for its own sake". The essay's version: knowledge becomes
code, code produces knowledge, and both take part in revising their own
representations. Even then the system is called a reflective programming
substrate, and counts as a strange loop only once the cross-level
self-reference has been demonstrated.

### Output as new source

Because output may be Markdown, a result can contain headings, links and new
cells. Data becomes code, which becomes data. That makes the loop more
capable and more dangerous, and is why rendering, integration and execution
are kept as separate steps in [[render-integrate-execute]].

## Relations

- It is a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: is-a} made of cards, cells and revisions.
- It is not yet a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: the cycle has no change of level by itself.
- What it would need is a [[level-crossing]] {relation: uses}, a card that represents its own interpreter.
- Its smallest test is the [[observer-card-experiment]] {relation: generalizes}.
- Its safety discipline is [[render-integrate-execute]] {relation: uses}.
- Its interpretation step is held by a [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses} or a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: uses}.
- Its closing step is [[revision]] {relation: uses}, recorded as a commit.
- It mirrors the [[reasoning-cycle]] {relation: analogous-to}: run, observe, revise.
- It is this vault's answer in progress to [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[feedback-loop]].** Output influencing later input, with no change of
  level. It is what this loop is until shown otherwise.
- **[[observer-card-experiment]].** A single card that links to itself and
  is inspected by its own cell at a pinned revision. It is the minimal case
  in which the condition above can be tested.
- **[[render-integrate-execute]].** Three operations on generated content,
  each requiring its own decision. It is what keeps this loop from running
  itself.
- **[[level-crossing]].** Treating something at one level of description as
  belonging to another. It is the missing ingredient.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[hmd-feedback-loop]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[hmd-feedback-loop]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[find-level-crossings]] is the
attempt to check the condition above on this vault's own link cycles.

